{"code":"51-2051.00","title":"Fiberglass Laminators and Fabricators","description":"Laminate layers of fiberglass on molds to form boat decks and hulls, bodies for golf carts, automobiles, or other products.","jobZone":2,"kinds":{"R":{"score":6.74,"share":0.54},"I":{"score":2.02,"share":0.096},"A":{"score":2.22,"share":0.1148},"S":{"score":1,"share":0},"E":{"score":1.16,"share":0.0151},"C":{"score":3.49,"share":0.2342}},"highPoints":["R","C"],"tasks":[{"id":13970,"text":"Release air bubbles and smooth seams, using rollers.","core":true,"importance":4.55,"relevance":99.78,"frequency":5.47},{"id":13971,"text":"Spray chopped fiberglass, resins, and catalysts onto prepared molds or dies using pneumatic spray guns with chopper attachments.","core":true,"importance":4.47,"relevance":75.99,"frequency":5.38},{"id":13980,"text":"Apply layers of plastic resin to mold surfaces prior to placement of fiberglass mats, repeating layers until products have the desired thicknesses and plastics have jelled.","core":false,"importance":4.44,"relevance":56.59,"frequency":5.53},{"id":13974,"text":"Mix catalysts into resins, and saturate cloth and mats with mixtures, using brushes.","core":true,"importance":4.43,"relevance":99.04,"frequency":5.16},{"id":13976,"text":"Check completed products for conformance to specifications and for defects by measuring with rulers or micrometers, by checking them visually, or by tapping them to detect bubbles or dead spots.","core":true,"importance":4.38,"relevance":82.18,"frequency":5.43},{"id":13973,"text":"Pat or press layers of saturated mat or cloth into place on molds, using brushes or hands, and smooth out wrinkles and air bubbles with hands or squeegees.","core":true,"importance":4.29,"relevance":93.42,"frequency":5.28},{"id":13984,"text":"Inspect, clean, and assemble molds before beginning work.","core":false,"importance":4.29,"relevance":65.38,"frequency":4.46},{"id":13972,"text":"Select precut fiberglass mats, cloth, and wood-bracing materials as required by projects being assembled.","core":true,"importance":4.22,"relevance":85.1,"frequency":5.15},{"id":13979,"text":"Cure materials by letting them set at room temperature, placing them under heat lamps, or baking them in ovens.","core":false,"importance":4.18,"relevance":63.61,"frequency":5.59},{"id":13982,"text":"Apply lacquers and waxes to mold surfaces to facilitate assembly and removal of laminated parts.","core":false,"importance":4.07,"relevance":56.73,"frequency":4.29},{"id":13975,"text":"Bond wood reinforcing strips to decks and cabin structures of watercraft, using resin-saturated fiberglass.","core":true,"importance":4.02,"relevance":85.55,"frequency":4.74},{"id":13978,"text":"Repair or modify damaged or defective glass-fiber parts, checking thicknesses, densities, and contours to ensure a close fit after repair.","core":false,"importance":4.02,"relevance":61.43,"frequency":4.54},{"id":13981,"text":"Mask off mold areas not to be laminated, using cellophane, wax paper, masking tape, or special sprays containing mold-release substances.","core":false,"importance":4.02,"relevance":65.59,"frequency":4.07},{"id":13983,"text":"Check all dies, templates, and cutout patterns to be used in the manufacturing process to ensure that they conform to dimensional data, photographs, blueprints, samples, or customer specifications.","core":false,"importance":3.87,"relevance":63.96,"frequency":4.82},{"id":13977,"text":"Trim excess materials from molds, using hand shears or trimming knives.","core":true,"importance":3.82,"relevance":68.66,"frequency":5.03},{"id":13985,"text":"Trim cured materials by sawing them with diamond-impregnated cutoff wheels.","core":false,"importance":3.68,"relevance":60.7,"frequency":3.91}],"activities":[{"id":"4.A.1.b.2","importance":4.12},{"id":"4.A.1.a.1","importance":4.07},{"id":"4.A.2.b.1","importance":3.87},{"id":"4.A.1.b.1","importance":3.73},{"id":"4.A.1.a.2","importance":3.67},{"id":"4.A.3.a.3","importance":3.64},{"id":"4.A.2.a